{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"premise":"The target's central mechanistic claim — that urban materials have lower emissivity than rural surfaces, causing slower urban nocturnal cooling — is factually backwards. Common urban construction materials (asphalt, concrete, brick) have emissivities of ~0.90–0.97, comparable to or higher than many natural surfaces, especially dry bare soils, sands, and rocks in arid regions (often ~0.85–0.93). The target itself even characterizes rural arid surfaces as having 'high emissivity,' then asserts urban surfaces have 'lower effective emissivity,' a claim contradicted by standard material radiative property data. Since emissivity is the load-bearing mechanism offered for the temperature gap, the explanation is unsound on its face.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864","quote":"rural surfaces with high emissivity and adequate moisture cool rapidly after sunset, whereas urban surfaces exhibit lower effective emissivity, causing the city to retain heat longer and cool more slowly."}],"case":"The explanation is grounded in a reversed physical premise. If urban emissivity is not systematically lower than rural emissivity — and standard data show asphalt (~0.90–0.98), concrete (~0.92–0.96), and brick (~0.90–0.93) are not lower than, and often exceed, the emissivity of dry natural soils, sands, and rocks typical of arid rural surroundings — then the stated causal chain (lower urban emissivity → slower urban cooling → nocturnal UHI) does not hold. The counterexample is not exotic: it is the very arid-city case the target highlights, where rural dry soil/sand/rock surfaces commonly have emissivities in the same range as urban materials, undermining the claim that emissivity differences drive the gap.","counterexample":["Emissivity of dry sand: ~0.90; emissivity of weathered asphalt: ~0.93; emissivity of dry sandy soil: ~0.90; emissivity of concrete: ~0.94. In the arid-city case the target itself emphasizes, rural surfaces do not have systematically higher emissivity than urban surfaces, so the proposed emissivity mechanism cannot produce the claimed slower urban cooling."]}]}